  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 610 PM EST FRI NOV 6 2026\n\nThe long wave pattern will be amplifying through the extended\nforecast period. A parade of northern stream energy will maintain\nbroader cyclonic flow over southeastern Canada and New England \ninto early next week. A deep trough will then be nearing the West \nCoast from the eastern Pacific through the middle of next week. \nThis allows for sharper ridging to take shape across the \nContinental Divide, with an expansive and deep trough evolving \neast of the Plains, bringing well below normal temperatures to \nmost locations east of the Mississippi River after Wednesday.\n\nDrier weather will hold on through Wednesday, with temperatures\ngradually moderating through Tuesday. Overnight lows will average\naround 30 degrees in our most sheltered valleys Sunday night,\ngiven the clear skies and light winds in place. The blended \nguidance continues to be bullish with the lows west of I-75, but \nmodifications were made to align more with the COOP MOS trends and\nwhat we typically see, given this pattern. Highs will be\nmoderating from the upper 50s to lower 60s on Sunday, to mid and\nupper 60s by Tuesday. Lows will also follow suit, with widening\nridge/valley splits heading into Wednesday morning. A cold front\nwill pass through Wednesday into Wednesday night, but moisture \nlooks limited, so mainly an increase in cloud cover is expected. \nAs 500 mb heights lower across the Ohio and Tennessee valleys \nthrough Friday, some bouts of moisture in low level northwest flow\nmay allow for some light shower activity at times.\n\nTemperatures will be the bigger story, with highs retreating to\nthe lower and middle 50s by Friday. While confidence is lower on\nthe details this far out, whatever locations that miss out on the\nfreezing temperatures this weekend, will likely not be able to \navoid seeing freezing temperatures by next weekend or perhaps just\nbeyond if model trends hold.",
